%[text] %[text:anchor:T_AAC35072] # Lab 2: Vehicle Transfer Function Model
%[text] **Learning Goals**
%[text] - Derive the transfer function of a first order system.
%[text] - Compare the transfer function and virtual vehicle responses.
%[text] - Identify the model parameters for the virtual vehicle. \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] While designing a controller, you typically do not use the real system. Instead, you identify a model of the system and use it to design a suitable controller. In this lab, you will construct a transfer function model of a virtual vehicle. You will validate the model and analyze how it behaves in the presence of a disturbance (driving up a hill).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_30C42416] ## First order transfer function model
%[text] In a very simple representation, a vehicle can be modeled as a mass with two forces acting on it. The engine generates a force in the forward direction. In the opposite direction, a frictional force slows the vehicle down.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![cc\_fbd\_flat.png](text:image:2867)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Diagram of a simple vehicle model driving on a flat road*
%[text] In this model, the variables and parameters are:
%[text] - $x$, $\\dot x$, $\\ddot x$: the position (m), velocity (m/s), and acceleration (m/s$^2$) of the vehicle in the direction of travel, respectively
%[text] - $u$: traction force generated by the engine (N)
%[text] - $m$: vehicle mass (kg)
%[text] - $b$: coefficient of friction (N$\\cdot$s/m) \
%[text] The differential equation model is constructed using Newton's second law:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m\\ddot{x} = -b\\dot{x}  + u$    (1)
%[text] The traction force is considered to be the input to the system and is therefore labeled $u$. A cruise control system attempts to determine the value of $u$ to achieve a desired response.
%%
