%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A912C11E] ### ![write.png](text:image:70b7) 3. System identification
%[text] In the previous part of the lab, you compared the step responses of the transfer function model and the virtual vehicle. The responses were similar, but the systems clearly had different parameters. In this part, you will identify the transfer function parameters that model the virtual vehicle using the step response.
%[text] **First order transfer function time constant**
%[text] A first order transfer function can be written in terms of its limiting value $h\_\\infty$ and time constant $\\tau$ (the time taken to reach 63% of the limiting value). 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $H(s) = \\frac{h\_\\infty }{\\tau s + 1}$
%[text] You can analytically compute the transfer function step response of $H$ to be:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $h(t) = h\_\\infty(1 - e^{-t /\\tau})$
%[text] From the step response it is easy to show that 
%[text] - $\\lim\_{t \\rightarrow \\infty} h(t) = h\_\\infty$ and 
%[text] - $h(\\tau) = h\_\\infty(1 - e^{-1})  \\approx 0.632 h\_\\infty$. \
%[text] These two properties show that $h\_\\infty$ is the limiting value of the step response and $\\tau$ is the time required to reach 63% of the limiting value.
%%
